textosLlegan("theory", {"fil-t7":{"html":"<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>How to study this topic: read each section slowly and underline the <strong>terms in bold</strong> (they are the exam vocabulary). At the end of each block, try to answer in your own words the question that heads it. The tables—objective/subjective beauty, the theories of art, Plato/Aristotle, moralism/autonomism—are the best guide for revision. And above all: look at works and images while you study; aesthetics is thought through with examples in front of you.</p> </blockquote>\n<h2>1. What is aesthetics?</h2>\n<p>Every day we judge things by their appearance, their sound or what they make us feel: a landscape strikes us as beautiful, a song moves us, a film gives us food for thought. <strong>Aesthetics</strong> is the <strong>branch of philosophy devoted to thinking about beauty, art and our experience of both</strong>. Its task is not to produce works or give painting lessons: it is devoted to <strong>reflecting</strong> on what beauty is, what turns something into a work of art and what happens within us when something moves us.</p>\n<p>The word comes from the Greek <strong>aisthesis</strong>, meaning <strong>sensation, perception through the senses</strong>. It was chosen by the philosopher <strong>Alexander Baumgarten</strong> in the 18th century to name the study of <strong>sensory knowledge</strong> (that which passes through the senses and emotions), as opposed to the logical knowledge of reason. That is why aesthetics deals with a special kind of experience, the <strong>aesthetic experience</strong>: a way of perceiving in which we do not seek to use the thing or to know what it is for, but <strong>stop to contemplate it</strong> for its own sake.</p>\n<p>Aesthetics asks itself <strong>two great questions</strong>, which organise the whole topic: <strong>what is the beautiful?</strong> (does beauty exist? is it in things or in the one who looks?) and <strong>what is art?</strong> (what turns something into a work of art?). It is also related to almost everything: to <strong>ethics</strong> (can a beautiful work be immoral?), <strong>politics</strong> (committed art, propaganda), <strong>religion</strong> (sacred art), <strong>science</strong> (is there beauty in a formula?) and today, above all, to the <strong>image</strong> and the <strong>audiovisual culture</strong> that surrounds us.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t7-55\"><strong>The symbolic animal.</strong> The classical definition says that the human being is a “rational animal”. Ernst Cassirer (<em>An Essay on Man</em>, 1944) proposed another one that fits this topic better: we are, above all, a <strong>symbolic animal</strong>. Two things need to be told apart. A <strong>signal</strong> has a single meaning and triggers an almost automatic reaction: dark clouds announce rain, and the dog that hears the lead runs to the door. A <strong>symbol</strong>, by contrast, allows many meanings and calls for interpretation: a cross means one thing to a Christian and another to someone who does not know that tradition. The Basque word “mendi” does not look like a mountain; it means mountain because a community has agreed that it should. Language is the great system of symbols, and art is its closest relative: a painting, a melody or a dance represents reality symbolically and awakens emotions, ideas and values. That is why animals simply receive the world, whereas we also build it with symbols.</p>\n<p>Aesthetics does not stay in museums. We choose clothes, hairstyles or music to say which group we belong to: the punks of the 1970s, with their mohicans and safety pins, sought out precisely what most people called ugly, in order to set themselves apart. We buy objects as much for how they look as for what they do, and that is why <strong>design</strong> is now a huge part of the economy. And some people look for a biological basis for certain tastes: the philosopher Denis Dutton (<em>The Art Instinct</em>, 2009) gathered studies according to which people from very different cultures prefer open landscapes with water and scattered trees, perhaps because those were safe places for our ancestors. Other authors reply that these preferences are also learnt.</p>\n<p><strong>Aesthetics outside the museum.</strong> In 1896 the architect Louis Sullivan wrote that form ever follows function, and the Bauhaus, the school Walter Gropius founded in Weimar in 1919, set out to unite art, craft and industry: a chair or a lamp would be beautiful because it was well designed, without added ornament. It is the Socratic idea of the beautiful as what suits its purpose, turned into an industrial programme. Fashion works differently. Georg Simmel (<em>The Philosophy of Fashion</em>, 1905) saw that it satisfies two opposite desires, to be like others and to stand out from them, and that is why it never stops: once everyone is wearing something, those who wanted to stand out drop it. Even nature has an aesthetic history. That a mountain is a landscape, and not an obstacle, is something we learn: the philosopher Alain Roger spoke of <strong>artialisation</strong> (<em>A Short Treatise on Landscape</em>, 1997), because we look at the countryside with eyes trained by painting and photography; today many viewpoints already have a frame for taking the photo. The next step is the <strong>aestheticisation of everyday life</strong>: the plate is photographed before it is eaten, the bedroom is decorated so that it looks good on screen and one’s own face is touched up with filters. Politics does not escape either: rallies are staged, candidates are designed like brands and a well-edited video convinces more than a manifesto. This is what Benjamin feared when he spoke of aestheticising politics; the debate is whether in a media democracy it is unavoidable or a danger. The question in every case is the same: when everything has to be pretty, what room is left for what is not?</p>\n<p><strong>Cinema according to Deleuze.</strong> The French philosopher Gilles Deleuze devoted two books to cinema, <em>The Movement-Image</em> (1983) and <em>The Time-Image</em> (1985), starting from a striking idea: great film-makers think, not with concepts, as philosophers do, but with moving images. In classical cinema the <strong>movement-image</strong> dominates: a character perceives a situation, feels something and acts, and the action changes the situation. It is the pattern of the western or of almost any action film: someone threatens, the hero reacts and everything links up towards an ending. After the Second World War, Deleuze says, that pattern breaks. In Italian neorealism, for example in Roberto Rossellini’s <em>Stromboli</em> (1950) or <em>Journey to Italy</em> (1954), characters face situations that overwhelm them and do not know what to do: rather than act, they look. Then the <strong>time-image</strong> appears, in which what we see on screen is time itself: long shots in which almost nothing happens, memories that mix with the present, as in Alain Resnais’s <em>Last Year at Marienbad</em> (1961), where we never know whether what is remembered really happened. Orson Welles, with the depth of field of <em>Citizen Kane</em> (1941), had already opened up that path. The distinction helps us understand why a slow film can bore or fascinate us: it does not offer us action to follow, but time to inhabit. Pay attention to the next film or series you watch: what matters, what the characters do or what happens to them while they look?</p>\n<h2>2. The beautiful: is it in the object or in the subject?</h2>\n<p>When we say ‘this is beautiful’, do we describe a <strong>property of the thing</strong> or do we express <strong>something that happens to us</strong>? Here two great conceptions clash.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t7-8\"><strong>Objective beauty (the classical conception).</strong> For the Greeks, beauty is <strong>in the object</strong>: it is a matter of <strong>proportion, harmony, order and measure</strong>. The beautiful is the well-proportioned. The <strong>Pythagoreans</strong> believed that beauty is <strong>number</strong>: music sounds good when its intervals keep simple mathematical proportions. The sculptor <strong>Polykleitos</strong> wrote a <em>Canon</em> with the ideal proportions of the body. Centuries later, <strong>Saint Augustine</strong> and <strong>Saint Thomas</strong> follow this line: the beautiful is what has <strong>integrity, proportion and clarity</strong>, and pleases when contemplated. On this view, beauty can be <strong>measured</strong> and <strong>taught</strong>, because it is in things.</p>\n<h3>Beauty as a transcendental</h3><p>The classical and medieval tradition carried objective beauty to its strongest consequence. It called <strong>transcendentals</strong> certain properties that do not belong to one type of thing, as colour belongs to bodies, but to everything that is. They go beyond the categories and <em>are convertible with being</em>: wherever there is being, there they are. Every entity, by the mere fact of being, is <strong>one</strong>, <strong>true</strong> (intelligible) and <strong>good</strong> (desirable). Many authors in that tradition added the <strong>beautiful</strong>, although Thomas Aquinas does not expressly include it in his list and interpreters still debate this. The root is Platonic. In the <em>Symposium</em> (210a-212a), Diotima describes an ascent from a beautiful body to Beauty itself. In the <em>Phaedrus</em> (250d), beauty is the only Idea that shines through sight: it is the most manifest (<em>ekphanéstaton</em>) and the most lovable. Thomas takes up this heritage in the <em>Summa Theologiae</em>. The beautiful is what pleases when seen (I, q. 5, a. 4), and it requires integrity, proportion and clarity (I, q. 39, a. 8). From this something important follows: the ugly would have no essence of its own. It would be <strong>privation</strong>, a lack of order or measure, just as evil was for Augustine the absence of a due good. Think of an old carpenter’s plane, with its handle worn by decades of use. It is still beautiful because of its proportion and because it serves its purpose so well, and the wear is only an accident. Modern aesthetics breaks this link. For Kant, the satisfaction of the judgement of taste is <em>disinterested</em> (<em>Critique of Judgement</em>, §2) and does not depend on the good. Moreover, the ugly and the sublime acquire artistic value of their own.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: can something be beautiful and at the same time unjust or false? Faced with Munch’s <em>The Scream</em>, is ugliness only the absence of beauty or does it have a force of its own?</p>\n<p>For the Greeks, moreover, the beautiful and the good went together: the ideal was to be <em>kalòs kagathós</em>, beautiful and good at the same time. Plato took this into his theory of Forms: in the <em>Symposium</em>, Diotima explains that love begins with a beautiful body, rises to the beauty of souls, of laws and of knowledge, and ends by contemplating <strong>Beauty itself</strong>, which does not change and is very close to the Good. Modernity separated what Plato had united: Kant made aesthetic judgement something distinct from moral judgement, and in 1857 Baudelaire gave the title <em>The Flowers of Evil</em> to a book of poems that sought beauty precisely in what morality condemned.</p>\n<p>From this idea of beauty as number comes an often-repeated myth: that of the <strong>golden ratio</strong>, a ratio of approximately 1.618 that is supposedly hidden in the Parthenon, in the <em>Mona Lisa</em> or in the most beautiful face. The mathematician Mario Livio (<em>The Golden Ratio</em>, 2002) reviewed these claims and concluded that most of them do not hold up: with a little patience you can find any proportion in any building. And since Gustav Fechner carried out the first experiments in 1876, studies have failed to show that people clearly prefer golden rectangles.</p>\n<h3>Three arts, three materials</h3><p>All arts express, but each does so with different means. <strong>Literature</strong> works with language. Roman Jakobson, in “Linguistics and Poetics” (1960), called the <strong>poetic function</strong> the one that focuses attention on the message itself: its form, its rhythm, its images. He called the <strong>emotive function</strong> the one that expresses the state of the speaker. In an invented line such as “the wind whistles in the dry sierra”, the repetition of the <em>s</em> makes what is said audible: it is an alliteration. <strong>Painting</strong> uses plastic signs on a surface: line, colour, form, texture, composition and light. It can be figurative, if it represents something recognisable, or abstract. A painting of an empty room in blues and greys conveys solitude without any need for words. <strong>Music</strong> organises sounds and silences in time. It uses <em>melody</em>, which is a succession of sounds, <em>harmony</em>, which is simultaneous sounds, and <em>rhythm</em>, which organises durations and accents. When a cheerful song changes from the major to the minor mode, the mood darkens immediately, even if the lyrics do not change. In <em>Laocoön</em> (1766), <a href=\"#biografias/lessing\">Lessing</a> explained that the material of each art both limits it and opens up possibilities for it. Painting is an art of space: it shows bodies at once, in a single instant. Poetry is an art of time: it narrates actions that follow one another. Therefore a painting cannot tell a complete story as a poem can, and a poem does not show a face at a single glance as a painting can.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: can the same emotion be expressed in the same way in a poem, in a painting and in a melody? What is lost, and what is gained, when it is translated from one art to another?</p>\n<p>The classical view is not entirely homogeneous. For <strong>Socrates</strong>, beauty is linked to the <strong>useful</strong>: a thing is beautiful if it fits its <strong>function</strong> well (thus, a utensil is handsome when it correctly performs what it is for). <strong>Aristotle</strong> also introduces a requirement concerning <strong>size</strong>: what is excessively tiny cannot be distinguished clearly, and what is disproportionate cannot be grasped in a single glance. <strong>Plotinus</strong>, for his part, denies that the beautiful can be reduced to proportion, since there are also simple realities that are beautiful, such as a colour or light: sensible things possess beauty insofar as they <strong>participate</strong> in a higher Beauty, proper to the intelligible world, of which they are a reflection. This thesis would later pass into the aesthetics of the Middle Ages.</p>\n<p><strong>Subjective beauty (the modern conception).</strong> From the Modern Age onwards the opposite idea gains strength: beauty <strong>is not in the object, but in the subject</strong> who contemplates it. ‘Beauty is in the eye of the beholder.’ The beautiful is not a property like weight or colour: it is the <strong>pleasure</strong> we feel before something. Hence the famous ‘there is no accounting for taste’. As we shall see, <strong>Hume</strong> and <strong>Kant</strong> will try, each in his own way, to save this idea without falling into total relativism.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr> <th></th> <th><strong>Objective beauty (classical)</strong></th> <th><strong>Subjective beauty (modern)</strong></th> </tr> </thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr> <td>Where is beauty?</td> <td>In the object</td> <td>In the subject who perceives it</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>What it consists of</td> <td>Proportion, harmony, order, measure</td> <td>Pleasure, feeling, taste</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Can it be measured?</td> <td>Yes (number, canon)</td> <td>Not entirely (it depends on who looks)</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Authors</td> <td>Pythagoreans, Polykleitos, Augustine, Thomas</td> <td>Hume, Kant (with nuances)</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t7-12\"><strong>Other aesthetic categories.</strong> The beautiful is not the only thing we value: there is also <strong>the sublime</strong> (what overwhelms us by being immense or terrible: a storm, the sea, the cosmos), <strong>the ugly</strong>, <strong>the tragic</strong>, <strong>the comic</strong> or <strong>the grotesque</strong>. And we must distinguish the <strong>beautiful</strong> from the merely <strong>agreeable</strong>: an ice cream is agreeable (it satisfies an appetite), but we do not call it ‘beautiful’. The beautiful <strong>pleases without satisfying a desire</strong>.</p>\n<p>The consideration of the sublime as an independent category is due to Edmund Burke, who in 1757 published the essay <em>A Philosophical Enquiry into the Origin of Our Ideas of the Sublime and Beautiful</em>. According to this author, the beautiful seduces us through its smallness, its smoothness and its delicacy, and arouses affection in us; the sublime, by contrast, arises from that which is capable of harming us (the storm, the precipice, darkness, the disproportionate), provided we observe it from safety: in that situation fear is transformed into a kind of intense enjoyment. Later Kant took up this difference in his <em>Critique of Judgement</em> (1790), and Romantic painters, such as Caspar David Friedrich with his landscapes, transferred it to canvas.</p>\n<p><strong>Nietzsche: art as the affirmation of life.</strong> In <em>The Birth of Tragedy</em> (1872), Friedrich Nietzsche explained Greek art through the tension between two forces, which he named after gods. The <strong>Apollonian</strong> (from Apollo) is form, measure, clarity and the dream of beautiful images; the <strong>Dionysian</strong> (from Dionysus) is intoxication, passion, chaos and merging with life. Great Greek tragedy, he argued, was born from joining the two. With Nietzsche, art stops being merely pleasant and harmonious: it is a way of saying yes to life with all its force and contradictions, including what is terrible in it. It is one more step towards a beauty that can be strange, provocative or uncomfortable, like the one the avant-gardes of the twentieth century would seek.</p>\n<h2>3. The judgement of taste: Hume and Kant</h2>\n<p>If beauty is subjective, a problem arises: when I say ‘this work is beautiful’, am I saying something that others ought to agree with, or only ‘I like it’? We call the judgement that expresses this evaluation a <strong>judgement of taste</strong> or <strong>aesthetic judgement</strong>.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t7-15\"><strong>David Hume.</strong> He recognises that taste is subjective, but does not believe that all judgements are worth the same. There is such a thing as <strong>good taste</strong>, and it belongs to the <strong>competent critic</strong>: someone with <strong>sensibility</strong>, <strong>experience</strong>, comparison between many works and <strong>free of prejudice</strong>. In this way, without denying subjectivity, Hume saves the idea that in art <strong>some judgements are better than others</strong>.</p>\n<p>To explain what delicacy of taste is, Hume tells a story he takes from <em>Don Quixote</em> (II, 13). Two of Sancho’s kinsmen taste the wine from a barrel: one finds a slight taste of iron in it and the other, of leather. Everyone laughs at them, until, when the barrel is emptied, a key tied to a leather strap appears at the bottom. Both were right: they noticed what was there and others did not perceive. Against this idea the saying ‘there is no accounting for taste’ is often quoted, which in Latin is <em>de gustibus non est disputandum</em>. It is not by any Roman author: it appears in the Middle Ages, and originally it referred above all to tastes in food.</p>\n<p>Hume admits, however, a limit: even among competent critics there remain disagreements that are due to no mistake, but to each person’s <strong>temperament</strong> and to the <strong>customs and beliefs</strong> of each age and country (a young person and an old one do not enjoy the same authors equally). That is why the argument from <strong>variation</strong> must be handled carefully: that tastes differ does not by itself prove that beauty is purely subjective, because we also disagree on questions that have an answer.</p>\n<p>A useful distinction can be drawn from Hume. Saying ‘I like it’ expresses a <strong>preference</strong>, and nobody can argue with you about it; saying ‘it is good’ is a <strong>judgement</strong>, and there reasons do come in: you can enjoy a film that you yourself admit is bad. That is why taste <strong>can be educated</strong>: someone who has listened to a lot of jazz hears things in a solo that a beginner does not. The problem is that this education seems circular: we learn from those who have good taste, but who decided that they had it? Besides, taste does not depend only on quality. The psychologist Robert Zajonc (1968) showed that the more times we see something, the more we like it, even if we do not remember having seen it: this is the <strong>mere-exposure effect</strong>. That is why a song you hated in June may end up appealing to you in September, after hearing it all summer.</p>\n<p><strong>There’s no arguing about taste?</strong> The proverb says so, but experience says otherwise. Just look at what happens in a group of friends the day after the last episode of a series: one says the ending was rushed; another, that it was the only possible one; a third, that the series had been boring for two seasons. We argue about films, songs, which contestant deserved to win or whether a new video game copies another. And we don’t stop at saying “I like it” or “I don’t”: we give reasons. We point out that a character changes their mind for no reason, that the chorus repeats a worn-out formula or that a film’s cinematography is flat. Those reasons can be examined: someone can watch the scene again and check whether it is true. If taste could not be argued about, all those conversations would be absurd, and they don’t seem so. But nor does it seem that they can be settled like a maths problem: nobody leaves the argument obliged to enjoy what used to bore them. That tension is the heart of this section. Montaigne will show how much tastes vary; Hume and Kant will look, each in his own way, for what they have in common; Bourdieu will ask where they come from; and Schiller, what educating them is for.</p>\n<p><strong>Montaigne: as many beauties as peoples.</strong> Michel de Montaigne (1533-1592) filled his <em>Essays</em> with reports of other peoples, taken from travellers’ books and the chronicles of the conquest of the Americas. In the longest chapter of the work, the “Apology for Raymond Sebond” (II, 12), he uses them to humble human reason, and one of his proofs is beauty: “It is likely that we hardly know what beauty is in nature and in general, since we give so many different forms to human beauty” (<em>Essays</em>, II, 12; own translation). If there were a natural rule, he adds, we would all recognise it, just as we all feel that fire is hot. And he lists: in the Indies they paint beauty black and dark-skinned, with thick, swollen lips and a flat, broad nose; in Peru, he says, the largest ears are the most beautiful, and people stretch them on purpose; there are peoples who blacken their teeth with great care and scorn to see them white; and in Mexico a small forehead counts among the beauties. Some of these reports are hearsay, and Montaigne did not check them. But his argument does not depend on each detail: it is enough to look at our grandparents’ photos to see that here too, in two generations, the weight, skin or eyebrows considered beautiful have changed.</p>\n<p><strong>What people suffer to be beautiful.</strong> In another chapter, whose title is already a thesis, “That the taste of good and evil depends in large part on the opinion we have of them” (I, 14), Montaigne looks for proof that pain does not weigh the same for everyone: it depends on what we think we gain by suffering it. And once again he finds it in beauty. He recalls a woman in Paris who had herself flayed to get new, fresher skin; people who had healthy teeth pulled out to make their voice softer or to arrange the others better; and others who swallowed sand and ash, ruining their stomachs on purpose, to acquire the pallor that was then admired. On the corsets that gave a Spanish-style waist he writes: “What torment do they not suffer, laced and strapped [...] down to the living flesh? Yes, sometimes to the point of death” (I, 14; own translation). Montaigne does not want to mock: he wants to show the power of opinion, which can turn a torment into something desirable. The question is still alive. Today some people spend hours in the sun to get a tan, some never appear in a photo without a filter, and some diet to look like a body that often does not even exist outside the screen. Every age believes its standards of beauty are natural, and almost always they belong only to that age.</p>\n<p><strong>Hume: taste is a fact, and it has a standard.</strong> David Hume published “Of the Standard of Taste” in 1757, in his <em>Four Dissertations</em>. He begins by agreeing with Montaigne: the variety of tastes is obvious, and the sentiment of beauty lies in the beholder, not in the object. But there are sentiments we all consider absurd: anyone who said that a mediocre author of his day was as good as Milton would be defending something as extravagant as saying that a pond is as extensive as the ocean. For Hume, this means that we are looking for a <strong>standard of taste</strong>, a rule that reconciles people’s sentiments. And he looks for it like a scientist, in the facts. The first is the test of time: “The same Homer, who pleased at Athens and Rome two thousand years ago, is still admired at Paris and at London” (para. 11). Fashions pass; whatever survives changes of language, religion and customs has something that connects with human nature. The second fact is that of Sancho’s kinsmen, which you already know: one declared the wine good, “were it not for a small taste of leather” (para. 15). Hume adds that producing general rules of art, or acknowledged models, is like finding the key with the leather thong (para. 16): it does not improve the wine, but it makes it possible to convince those who laughed.</p>\n<p><strong>The portrait of the good judge.</strong> Who decides, then? Hume answers with a portrait and sums it up in one sentence: what is needed is “strong sense, united to delicate sentiment, improved by practice, perfected by comparison, and cleared of all prejudice” (para. 23). These are five distinct qualities. <strong>Delicacy</strong> is that of Sancho’s wine: noticing small nuances. <strong>Practice</strong> means returning to the same work: the first time you hear an album everything blurs, and by the third listen you can pick out the bass line. <strong>Comparison</strong> is having known many works from different periods and styles: someone who has read only one teen saga has nothing to measure it against. <strong>Freedom from prejudice</strong> requires putting yourself in the place of the audience the work was meant for, and not judging out of friendship or dislike for the author. And <strong>good sense</strong> is the reason that grasps the whole: how the parts fit together and whether the work achieves its purpose. Hume concludes that the joint verdict of those who combine these qualities is the true standard of taste, and he admits two awkward things: that such judges are rare even in the most refined ages, and that knowing who is one is also a question of fact, argued over with evidence. The objection of the circle, which you already know, returns here; Hume’s answer is that time and the facts break the circle.</p>\n<p><strong>Kant: what pleases only me.</strong> Kant takes a step that Hume had not taken: carefully separating two kinds of judgement that language mixes up. In § 7 of the <em>Critique of Judgement</em> (1790) he gives an example from his time: if someone says that Canary wine is agreeable, he does not mind being corrected and reminded that he should say “it is agreeable <em>to me</em>”. In the agreeable everyone has their own taste, the taste of the senses, and there the proverb does hold. With the beautiful it is different: “it would be ridiculous if someone who prided himself on his taste thought to justify himself by saying: this object [...] is beautiful for me” (§ 7; own translation). Whoever calls something beautiful speaks as if beauty were a property of the thing and expects others to agree; if they don’t, he does not say that everyone has their own taste, but that they lack it. And yet Kant denies that this agreement can be forced. In § 33 he explains that if a poem does not please him, no rule from the critics of his day, such as Batteux or Lessing, will convince him that it is beautiful: at most they will make him doubt his judgement, but he will have to read it again himself. That is why the judgement of taste demands everyone’s agreement but cannot be proved: it is argued over by pointing things out and inviting others to look again, not by counting votes or applying rules. Here Kant parts company with Hume, who looked for the standard in the verdict of good judges.</p>\n<p><strong>Bourdieu: taste as a social marker.</strong> Almost two centuries later, the French sociologist Pierre Bourdieu answered Kant from somewhere else. In <em>Distinction</em> (1979), whose subtitle, “a social critique of the judgement of taste”, deliberately echoes Kant’s title, he analysed surveys carried out in France in the 1960s on music, painting, cinema, food and home decoration. His thesis, in our own words: the taste we experience as personal and spontaneous is learnt at home and at school, and is distributed according to social class. Bourdieu spoke of <strong>cultural capital</strong>: the knowledge, habits and qualifications that are inherited and accumulated almost like money. Someone who grows up surrounded by books and museums learns early the gaze that values form for its own sake, Kant’s “disinterested” gaze, and that gaze requires time and distance from economic pressures. Other groups ask art to be good for something, to move them or to be well made. And taste, according to Bourdieu, does not only unite those who are alike: it also separates, because every judgement about a song or a film says something about the person judging and their place in society. It has been objected that his data come from one particular France, and that today many highly educated people mix opera with reggaeton: in the 1990s the sociologist Richard Peterson called them “cultural omnivores”. Even so, Bourdieu’s question is uncomfortable: when you say that something is “in bad taste”, are you talking about the work or about the people who enjoy it?</p>\n<p><strong>Educating taste.</strong> If we accept that taste is acquired, what we must consider is no longer whether it should be formed, but how to do so. Schiller, an author you have already encountered, wrote his series of letters <em>Über die ästhetische Erziehung des Menschen</em> in 1795, disappointed by the years of Terror that followed the French Revolution: no community attains freedom through laws if those who make it up have not previously exercised themselves in harmonising reason and sensibility. He sums it up with this formula: ‘it is through beauty that we arrive at freedom’ (Letter II; own translation). Today this can be interpreted in a simple and applicable way, combining the contributions of Hume, Kant and Bourdieu. Forming taste does not consist in memorising a list of works worthy of admiration, but in developing three habits. The first is <strong>exposure</strong>: listening, reading and observing in abundance, even that which you would not choose yourself, since it is impossible to assess something one has never had before one’s eyes. The second is <strong>comparison</strong>: placing side by side two interpretations of the same musical theme, or a novel and its film adaptation, and working out what distinguishes them. The third is <strong>reasons</strong>: explaining why a work succeeds or fails, in such a way that anyone else can verify it. Let us imagine someone who only listens to the songs suggested by the algorithm of their music platform. Their taste is not bad; it has simply been fixed by someone else. But if one day they investigate which old track contains the sample that repeats in their favourite song, and end up putting on a soul album from 1970, they have already begun to form their taste on their own initiative. They do not thereby obtain a correct opinion, but a greater margin of freedom when choosing.</p>\n<p><strong>Something to think about:</strong> think of a song, a series or a style of clothing that you used to find horrible and now like, or the other way round. What changed: the work, you or the people you hang out with? Now share out the change among the authors of this section. Was it a matter of practice and comparison, as Hume would say? Or because your group started to like it, as Bourdieu would say, or because you heard it a hundred times, as Zajonc showed? Think too of your last argument about a film or a singer. Did you give reasons the other person could check, or did you just say “I like it” and “I don’t”? If you had to persuade someone to watch a film you love, what would you tell them so that they would look at it differently, without forcing them? Finally, Montaigne: which standard of beauty today do you think will make people smile when they look at our photos in fifty years? And which of them is making someone suffer right now?</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t7-17\"><strong>Immanuel Kant.</strong> In his <em>Critique of Judgement</em> he offers the most influential analysis. The aesthetic judgement is: <strong>disinterested</strong> (I contemplate the thing <strong>without wanting to possess or use it</strong>); <strong>universal without a concept</strong> (I <strong>claim</strong> that others should find it beautiful, but <strong>I cannot prove it with rules</strong>: beauty is not proved like a theorem); with <strong>purposiveness without purpose</strong> (the work seems ordered ‘for something’, but it <strong>serves</strong> no practical end); and it produces a <strong>free pleasure</strong>. Kant also distinguishes <strong>free beauty</strong> (that of a flower, which pleases through its pure form) from <strong>adherent beauty</strong> (that of a building, which depends on what it is for), and reserves a place for <strong>the sublime</strong>: the feeling—a mixture of fear and admiration—before the <strong>immense</strong> or the <strong>powerful</strong>, which reminds us at once of our physical smallness and of the greatness of our reason, capable of thinking the infinite.</p>\n<h2>4. What is art? The great theories</h2>\n<p>Defining art is harder than it seems: what do a cathedral, a symphony, a poem and a urinal signed by Duchamp have in common? Throughout history several answers have been proposed.</p>\n<ul> <li><strong>Art as imitation (mimesis).</strong> Art <strong>imitates</strong> or <strong>represents</strong> reality; a painting is good if it ‘looks like’ what it portrays. It dominates from Greece to the Renaissance. Problem: some art <strong>imitates nothing</strong> (music, architecture, abstract art).</li> <li><strong>Art as expression.</strong> With <strong>Romanticism</strong>, art stops imitating the world and <strong>expresses the inner world</strong> of the artist. The work is valuable for what it <strong>communicates</strong> and <strong>makes us feel</strong>. Problem: a cry or a scream also expresses emotions, and they are not art.</li> <li><strong>Art as form (formalism).</strong> What is artistic is neither what it represents nor what the author feels, but its <strong>form</strong>: composition, colour, rhythm, structure. That is why there can be abstract art. Problem: it leaves out content and meaning.</li> <li><strong>The institutional theory (Danto, Dickie).</strong> Something is art not because of what it is like, but because <strong>the art world</strong> (museums, critics, art history) <strong>recognises and treats it as art</strong>. Problem: it seems that then ‘art is whatever the experts say’.</li> </ul>\n<p>Two twentieth-century authors gave precise form to two of those theories. The English critic Clive Bell (<em>Art</em>, 1914) argued that what all works of art have in common is <strong>significant form</strong>: a combination of lines and colours that arouses a special aesthetic emotion in whoever looks at it. The objection is that the definition goes round in a circle: significant form is what arouses aesthetic emotion, and aesthetic emotion is what significant form arouses. In 1938, in <em>The Principles of Art</em>, Robin George Collingwood defended a version of the expression theory and separated it from <strong>craft</strong>: the craftsman knows in advance what he is going to make, like someone building a chair from a plan, whereas the artist discovers what he feels by expressing it. For Collingwood, the real work is in the artist’s imagination, and the painting or the score is only its trace. His critics then ask where the work is in a film, made by hundreds of people, or in a cathedral that took centuries to build.</p>\n<p><strong>Clive Bell and significant form.</strong> Bell was writing in London, within the Bloomsbury Group, and his book had a specific opponent: the public that valued a painting only for the story it told. In 1910 his friend, the critic Roger Fry, gathered works by Cézanne, Van Gogh and Gauguin in a London exhibition, and much of the press responded with ridicule: those lopsided apples and flat colours looked like clumsiness. Bell replied with a question: what quality is shared by all the works that move us as works, from a Byzantine mosaic to a painting by Cézanne? His answer was <strong>significant form</strong>: certain relations of lines, colours, volumes and spaces that arouse in the sensitive viewer an <strong>aesthetic emotion</strong>, different from the joy, fear or nostalgia of ordinary life. Everything else is superfluous. Bell put it bluntly: “the representative element in a work of art may or may not be harmful; always it is irrelevant” (<em>Art</em>, Part I, ch. 1). For him, a painting such as William Frith’s <em>The Railway Station</em> at Paddington, full of travellers, luggage and anecdotes, is a curious document of its time, but not a work of art: it interests us for what it tells, not for its form.</p>\n<p><strong>What it leaves out and why it was useful.</strong> If form is what matters, the subject makes no difference: a crucifixion, a battle or a plate of apples are equally good starting points. Nor do the story being told, the skill in copying reality or the memories the painting stirs from our own lives count: according to Bell, to appreciate a work we need bring nothing from life. That exclusion was its strength. It made it possible to defend Cézanne, who deliberately altered perspective in order to build the picture out of volumes and colours, and it left room for abstract painting, which was being born in precisely those years. It also made it possible to admire the art of very distant cultures and periods, such as Byzantine mosaics or archaic sculpture, without knowing the beliefs of those who made it. The idea recalls Kant’s free beauty, that of the flower which pleases by its pure form. But the price is high: Bell has to say that anyone who shudders before <em>Guernica</em> because of what it tells about the bombing is not looking at it as a work of art, and that the lyrics of a song add nothing to its artistic value.</p>\n<p><strong>The circularity objection, more slowly.</strong> We have already seen that Bell’s definition goes round in circles. Let us see why that is serious. A good definition gives us a criterion for deciding doubtful cases: if I want to know whether an animal is a mammal, I check whether it suckles its young, without taking the result for granted. With Bell there is nothing of the kind. How do I know that a form is significant? Because it gives me aesthetic emotion. And how do I know that my emotion is aesthetic, and not mere fondness for the subject? Because a significant form produces it. Neither term can be checked without the other. Bell did not hide this: he wrote that every system of aesthetics must start from the personal experience of a peculiar emotion, and he trusted that sensitive people recognise it. But that brings a second problem: if two viewers disagree in front of a painting, the one who feels nothing can only be accused of lacking sensitivity, and the discussion is over. It is the opposite of what Hume sought with his competent critic, whose reasons could be examined. Some of Bell’s defenders reply that the circle is not vicious: we also learn what “red” is by pointing at red things, with no other definition. It remains an open question whether aesthetic emotion can be pointed out as easily as a colour.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t7-75\"><strong>Collingwood: to express is to clarify.</strong> Robin George Collingwood, an Oxford philosopher and historian, published <em>The Principles of Art</em> in 1938. He starts from a common experience: sometimes we feel something strong and confused, an unease we cannot name. The artist’s work, he says, consists in getting out of that confusion: by searching for the right words, sounds or forms, the artist discovers exactly what he or she feels. That is why expression is not letting off steam, like a scream or tears, which merely discharge the emotion, nor is it a description: whoever says “I am sad” files their state under a general label, whereas a good poem shows this sadness, the sadness of this moment, with its mixture of anger and tiredness, unlike any other. Collingwood calls this <strong>art proper</strong> (<em>art proper</em> in his own words; Book I, chs. VI and VII). The audience does not receive a ready-made emotion, as one receives a parcel: in understanding the work, they also clarify something of what they themselves feel. That is why Collingwood saw the audience as the artist’s collaborator, and not as a customer.</p>\n<p><strong>Three things that resemble art.</strong> Collingwood devotes a good part of the book to what we call art without its being quite art. The first is <strong>craft</strong>: a trade that aims at a result fixed before starting, with a plan, some means and a material that is transformed. The second is <strong>magical art</strong>, which arouses emotions for a practical end: the anthem that unites a stadium, the military march that gives courage, the rally song that mobilises voters. It is not to be despised, he says, because every society needs it, but its value lies in what it achieves outside the work. The third is <strong>amusement art</strong>, which arouses emotions so that they are spent there and then, in a world of make-believe and without consequences: the fright of a horror film, forgotten on leaving the cinema. Let us think of two songs. One is commissioned by a brand for an advert: the team knows what emotion it wants to provoke (cheerfulness, the urge to buy), what length it needs and at what second the chorus must come in. It is craft at the service of magic or amusement, and it can be excellent. The other is written by someone who cannot make sense of what has happened to them after a break-up, and who tries out lines until they hit on what they feel. Only the latter is, for Collingwood, art proper.</p>\n<p><strong>Objections to Collingwood.</strong> The first comes from collective and commissioned art. A Gothic altarpiece was often contracted in detail: the guild or the parish fixed the subject, the dimensions, the saints who had to appear and even the amount of gold. In Leipzig, from 1723 onwards, Bach composed cantatas for each Sunday’s services because it was his duty as director of music at St Thomas’s Church. If the end was fixed in advance, are they mere craft? Collingwood granted that art and craft can occur in the same work: a commission does not prevent the artist, in carrying it out, from clarifying something of their own. But then the boundary no longer separates works, but aspects of a work, and it is hard to tell which weighs more. The second objection is technique. If the essential thing is to clarify an emotion, what about the years of practice of a violinist or a draughtswoman? Collingwood does not despise it: without craft nothing can be expressed precisely. But he denies that technique is what makes a work art, and his critics reply that in music or dance it is hard to separate what one feels from what one knows how to do. The third is the usual one for theories of expression: we have no access to what the author felt, and a work can move us even if its author made it in cold blood.</p>\n<p><strong>Where each theory looks.</strong> Compared with the other theories in this topic, these two are distinguished above all by where they look for what is artistic. Imitation looks at the relation between the work and the world. Bell looks at the work alone, its lines and colours, and that allows him to defend what imitation condemned. Collingwood looks inside the artist, at the process by which a confused emotion becomes clear, and that is why it makes no difference to him whether the work is figurative or abstract. The institutional theory looks outside, at the art world that decides what is exhibited. The first two share something the institutional theory abandons: they believe that art has a nature of its own that can be discovered. Faced with a Duchamp ready-made, Bell would have to look for significant form in a urinal, and Collingwood for a clarified emotion in a gesture that was above all a provocation; the institutional theory, by contrast, explains it effortlessly, but in exchange it stops saying what is valuable about it. None of them solves everything, and each sheds light on works that the others leave in shadow.</p>\n<p><strong>Case: a black square and a song.</strong> In December 1915, at an exhibition in Petrograd, Kazimir Malevich hung in the high corner of the room, the place where icons stood in Russian homes, a canvas with a black square on a white ground. For Bell, the case is almost a textbook example: there is no subject and no story, only a form and its relation to the ground; if someone feels aesthetic emotion before it, it is art, and if they do not, Bell has no further arguments to give them. Collingwood would ask something else: did Malevich clarify anything by painting it, or was it a gesture calculated to shock? He himself presented it as the zero point of a new painting, which sounds more like a manifesto than a clarified emotion, although we can only know this through the work and through what he wrote. Now take a song you have listened to a lot this year. With Bell you would attend only to the form: the melody, the rhythm, how it builds and how it resolves; the lyrics would not count. With Collingwood you would ask whether it is made for you to dance and buy tickets, or whether someone found in it the words for something they did not know how to say. Perhaps the answer is “both”, and that is already an interesting conclusion.</p>\n<p><strong>Something to think about:</strong> choose a work that really moves you: a painting, a song, a video game or a film scene. First describe it as Bell would, without talking about the subject or the story, using only forms, colours, sounds and rhythm. Then as Collingwood would: what confused emotion might its author have clarified in making it, or was it made to order for a specific end, such as selling, cheering people up or frightening them? Compare the two descriptions. Which one better explains why it moves you? Has something important been left out of each? And one last question: if a song is composed by a team and for money, but its authors commit themselves to it until they discover something they did not know, does it stop being craft or does it remain so? Where would you draw the line between a trade and art?</p>\n<table data-bloque=\"fil-t7-21\">\n<thead>\n<tr> <th><strong>Theory</strong></th> <th>Art is…</th> <th>Fits with</th> <th>Struggles to explain</th> </tr> </thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr> <td>Imitation (mimesis)</td> <td>representing reality</td> <td>classical painting, sculpture</td> <td>music, abstract art</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Expression</td> <td>communicating the author’s emotion</td> <td>Romanticism, expressionism</td> <td>why any outburst is not art</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Form</td> <td>a structure beautiful in itself</td> <td>abstract art, design</td> <td>meaning, subject matter</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Institutional</td> <td>what the art world recognises</td> <td>avant-gardes, ready-mades</td> <td>risk of ‘art = whatever the experts say’</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<p>It is also worth distinguishing <strong>art</strong> from <strong>craft</strong> and from <strong>technique</strong>: the Greek word <strong>techne</strong> and the Latin <strong>ars</strong> originally meant ‘know-how’ (including that of the shoemaker or the doctor). Only in the Modern Age were the <strong>fine arts</strong> (painting, sculpture, music, poetry, architecture, and later dance and cinema) separated off as those whose purpose is not useful but <strong>aesthetic</strong>, and the figure of the <strong>genius</strong> appears: the original artist who <strong>creates new rules</strong> instead of merely following them.</p>\n<p>Another classic question is who decides the <strong>meaning</strong> of a work. For <strong>intentionalism</strong>, to interpret it well is to grasp what the artist meant to do; <strong>anti-intentionalism</strong> replies that intention is irrelevant: only the work as it is offered counts. But there are cases, such as the irony of many contemporary works, that cannot be understood without knowing what their author intended. At the same time, intention does not control the <strong>effects</strong> of the work in each context, and the spectator is not a passive receiver: they <strong>recreate</strong> the work in interpreting it, and some authors maintain that they share with the artist part of the <strong>responsibility</strong> for its meaning.</p>\n<p>Anti-intentionalism has named authors: in 1946 the critic William K. Wimsatt and the philosopher Monroe Beardsley gave the name <strong>intentional fallacy</strong> to judging a poem by what its author meant to say. For them, the intention is not visible and is not a reliable criterion: if the poet achieved what he wanted, it can be seen in the poem; if he did not, the poem does not contain it.</p>\n<h2>5. Art and truth: does art know?</h2>\n<p>Does art merely entertain, or does it also <strong>tell us something true</strong> about the world? Philosophy has answered in opposite ways.</p>\n<p><strong>Plato: distrust.</strong> Since the sensible world is already a copy of the <strong>Ideas</strong>, and art imitates the sensible world, the work is a <strong>copy of a copy</strong>: it takes us <strong>twice</strong> away from the truth. It also appeals to the <strong>emotions</strong> and to the irrational part of the soul, and can corrupt citizens. That is why, in his ideal city, Plato <strong>polices and even expels</strong> certain poets: art, for him, <strong>deceives</strong>.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t7-27\"><strong>Aristotle: rehabilitation.</strong> <strong>Mimesis</strong> is natural and pleasant (we learn by imitating), and tragedy, far from corrupting, produces <strong>catharsis</strong>: by feeling pity and fear at what happens on stage, the spectator <strong>discharges and purifies</strong> those emotions. Art, moreover, shows what <strong>could</strong> happen (the universal); that is why, Aristotle says, poetry is ‘more philosophical’ than history. Art <strong>does</strong> provide knowledge.</p>\n<p><strong>Hegel and after.</strong> For <strong>Hegel</strong>, art is the <strong>sensory manifestation of truth</strong> (of the Idea), but in the modern world it has ceased to be the main route to it (religion and philosophy are): this is the thesis of the <strong>‘end’ or ‘death’ of art</strong> (not that it disappears, but that it loses its central role). In the 20th century, <strong>Heidegger</strong> and <strong>Gadamer</strong> give art enormous value again: the work is an <strong>event of truth</strong>, a way in which the world <strong>opens up</strong> to us. And <strong>Adorno</strong> sees in authentic art a form of <strong>critical knowledge</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr> <th><strong>Author</strong></th> <th>Does art know the truth?</th> <th>Key idea</th> </tr> </thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr> <td>Plato</td> <td>No: it deceives (copy of a copy)</td> <td>Police/expel the poets</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Aristotle</td> <td>Yes: mimesis + catharsis</td> <td>Poetry shows the universal</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Hegel</td> <td>Yes, but it belongs to the past</td> <td>The ‘end of art’</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Heidegger/Gadamer</td> <td>Yes: the work opens up the world</td> <td>Art as the event of truth</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<h2>6. Art, emotions, culture and morality</h2>\n<h3>How does one create? Poincaré’s model</h3><p>We usually imagine inspiration as a bolt of lightning that falls from the sky onto the genius. The mathematician Henri Poincaré, in the lecture “Mathematical Creation”, included in <em>Science and Method</em> (1908), told how his discoveries came about, and his account belies that image. Four phases can be drawn from it. The first is <strong>preparation</strong>: weeks of conscious, intense work on a problem, often without result. It is followed by <strong>incubation</strong>: the effort is abandoned, and Poincaré supposed that the unconscious mind continued combining ideas. The third is <strong>illumination</strong>: the solution appears suddenly. He himself relates that, during an excursion, as he put his foot on the step of an omnibus in Coutances, he suddenly understood a key idea about the functions he was studying without thinking about them. Finally comes <strong>verification</strong>: back home, the idea is checked and developed consciously, because illumination can be mistaken. It was the psychologist Graham Wallas, in <em>The Art of Thought</em> (1926), who gave these phases their name and turned them into a general model. Although it was born of mathematics, it also applies to art. The lesson is clear: inspiration does not arise from nothing, but from prior work and from what we already know. This is captured in the saying of one per cent inspiration and ninety-nine per cent perspiration, usually attributed to Edison, although that attribution is doubtful. Imagine a pupil who gets stuck with an essay. She writes, crosses out, is not convinced by anything and goes to sleep. The next day, on the bus, the beginning she was looking for comes to her. In the afternoon she revises and corrects it.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: is the idea of the inspired genius compatible with the need to work? What role do breaks play in your way of studying and creating?</p>\n<p><strong>Art and emotions.</strong> Art is not only understood: it is <strong>felt</strong>. That emotional charge is part of its educational value: used well, art <strong>educates the feelings</strong> and widens our capacity to put ourselves in other people’s place (a novel makes us live other lives). That is why aesthetic competence seeks a <strong>balance between the rational and the emotional</strong>: neither analysing without feeling, nor feeling without thinking.</p>\n<p><strong>Friedrich Schiller</strong> radicalised this approach in the letters he devoted to the aesthetic education of the human being (<em>Über die ästhetische Erziehung des Menschen</em>). According to him, each person is divided between a sensuous tendency and a rational one, and only achieves full humanity in <strong>play</strong>, that free activity which reconciles the two impulses and culminates in art. Hence <strong>aesthetic education</strong> presents itself as the path leading to both ethical and political <strong>freedom</strong>: in order to transform the State, it is first necessary to educate citizens who know how to feel and reason in a harmonious way.</p>\n<p><strong>Ethics and aesthetics.</strong> Can a work be both <strong>beautiful and immoral</strong>? There are two positions: <strong>moralism</strong> (the moral values of a work affect its artistic value) and <strong>autonomism</strong> or ‘art for art’s sake’ (art has its <strong>own rules</strong>, independent of morality). Most people today adopt an intermediate path: morality <strong>can</strong> be relevant to judging a work, but it is <strong>not everything</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr> <th></th> <th><strong>Moralism</strong></th> <th><strong>Autonomism (‘art for art’s sake’)</strong></th> </tr> </thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr> <td>Does morality affect artistic value?</td> <td>Yes</td> <td>No</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Motto</td> <td>Art too answers to the good</td> <td>Art has its own laws</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Risk</td> <td>Turning art into a sermon or censorship</td> <td>Justifying any content</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<p>To go further: the reading <a href=\"#lecturas/fil-lec-aristoteles-wilde\">‘Aristotle and Wilde: what is art for?’</a> sets against each other two answers separated by more than two thousand years. For Aristotle (<em>Poetics</em>, ch. 4), imitating is natural to us, and we learn by enjoying imitations, even of things that in reality we would dislike seeing. For Oscar Wilde, in the preface he added in 1891 to his novel <em>The Picture of Dorian Gray</em>, art does not have to serve any purpose outside itself: it is the best-known defence of ‘art for art’s sake’.</p>\n<h2>7. The political role of art</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t7-36\">Art is never entirely innocent: whoever commissions, funds and exhibits a work has interests, and the work transmits (or questions) the values of its time. Hence an old debate: <strong>committed art</strong> (the artist must take sides and denounce injustice; art as a <strong>weapon</strong> of transformation) versus <strong>art for art’s sake</strong> (<strong>autonomy</strong>: art is not there to serve causes, but to be art).</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t7-37\">An extreme case is <strong>propaganda</strong>: the use of art and the image by <strong>power</strong> to manufacture consensus. <strong>Walter Benjamin</strong> summed it up: fascism <strong>aestheticises politics</strong> (turns it into a beautiful spectacle), and against this he proposed to <strong>politicise art</strong> (put it at the service of emancipation). <strong>Theodor Adorno</strong>, more pessimistic, distrusted propaganda and the <strong>culture industry</strong> (mass entertainment that lulls people to sleep) alike, and saw in difficult, autonomous art the last form of <strong>resistance</strong>. The conclusion is not simple: art <strong>can</strong> change people’s outlook (which is why dictatorships fear and censor it), but when placed at the direct service of a slogan it runs the risk of becoming a <strong>pamphlet</strong>.</p>\n<p>History offers examples on both sides, and sometimes in the same work. Goya engraved <em>The Disasters of War</em> (1810-1815) to show without embellishment the cruelty of both French and Spanish, and did not dare to publish them in his lifetime. Leni Riefenstahl filmed the Nazi party congress in Nuremberg in <em>Triumph of the Will</em> (1935) as a grandiose spectacle in the service of Hitler. And Picasso’s <em>Guernica</em>, painted after the bombing of the Biscayan town in April 1937, is at once a universal condemnation of war and a commission from the Government of the Republic for its pavilion at that year’s International Exhibition in Paris. That is why it is worth distinguishing the <strong>intention</strong> of the person who makes the work from the <strong>effects</strong> it has afterwards: <em>Guernica</em> still moves people who know nothing about that commission.</p>\n<p><strong>A long history.</strong> The political use of art did not begin with the dictatorships of the twentieth century. The Arch of Titus, put up in Rome around the year 81, shows in relief the spoils taken from the Temple in Jerusalem: it is an advertisement in stone for the emperor's power. Medieval cathedrals and their sculpture taught the faith to those who could not read and proclaimed the authority of the Church. In the twentieth century the Soviet Union imposed <strong>socialist realism</strong> from 1934, an art obliged to glorify the worker and the party; and in Spain the Franco regime censored books, films and plays for decades. But art has also been used to resist. The Mexican muralists (Diego Rivera, José Clemente Orozco, David Alfaro Siqueiros) painted the history of their people on public walls, and a mural Rivera made for the Rockefeller Center in New York was destroyed in 1934 because it included a portrait of Lenin. Today street art, with its graffiti and murals, is still often a form of protest. So art has two faces in politics: it strengthens power, and it also gives a voice to those who have none.</p>\n<p><strong>Art and censorship: the Hays Code and comics.</strong> Dictatorships are not the only ones that censor. In the United States, the film industry adopted a production code in 1930, known by the surname of Will Hays, who chaired the producers’ association. From 1934 it was enforced in earnest: no film was released in the major cinemas without the approval of an office run by Joseph Breen. The code banned showing crime sympathetically, ridiculing religion or the law, nudity and even overly passionate kisses; the criminal had to end up punished. It was not imposed by the government: the studios censored themselves to avoid boycotts and harsher laws. It lasted until 1968, when it was replaced by the age-rating system we still know. Something similar happened with comics. In the early fifties the publisher EC brought out horror and crime series such as <em>Crime SuspenStories</em>. In 1954, amid a panic about juvenile delinquency, the psychiatrist Fredric Wertham published <em>Seduction of the Innocent</em>, which blamed comics, and the Senate held hearings at which the publisher, William Gaines, had to defend a cover showing a severed head. That same year the publishers created the Comics Code, which banned gory horror and required good always to win. Most of EC’s series soon disappeared; the satirical magazine <em>MAD</em> survived, and decades later those stories inspired the television series <em>Tales from the Crypt</em> (1989). One can argue about whether this protected children or impoverished an art form, but it shows that censorship does not always arrive in uniform: sometimes the public asks for it and the market itself carries it out.</p>\n<p><strong>Who decides what can be shown?</strong> Today the debate has moved to a different stage. It helps to start with a distinction: when a government censors, it uses the law to punish whoever publishes; when a company removes content from its platform, it applies its own rules to those who use it. It is not the same thing, but when a few companies control almost the whole public conversation, the difference narrows. The case of X shows this: Elon Musk bought Twitter in October 2022, and later renamed it X, presenting himself as an absolute defender of freedom of expression; he reinstated banned accounts and relaxed moderation. Some celebrated it as the end of hidden censorship; others complained that hoaxes and hate speech grew as a result. So-called <strong>cancel culture</strong> raises the same issue from below: withdrawing support from an artist or a work because of what they have said or done. For some, it is the way a public that used to have no voice holds people to account; for others, it is censorship without a trial, in which a crowd decides within hours and without nuance. And there are cases that touch us from childhood: in the Brothers Grimm version of <em>Cinderella</em>, the stepsisters cut off a toe and a heel so that the shoe will fit, and at the wedding some doves peck out their eyes. Nobody bans those tales, although many present-day versions soften them. Why do we tolerate that violence and not another? Every 3 May, since the UN proclaimed it in 1993, World Press Freedom Day is marked, as a reminder that without the freedom to publish there can be no criticism. The underlying question remains open: if someone has to set limits, who should it be, and by what criterion?</p>\n<h2>8. Contemporary aesthetics: the image and audiovisual culture</h2>\n<p>The 20th century broke almost all certainties about art. The <strong>avant-gardes</strong> (Cubism, Dadaism, Surrealism, abstract art…) abandoned imitation and traditional beauty. The decisive blow was struck by <strong>Marcel Duchamp</strong> with the <strong>ready-made</strong>: taking any industrial object (a urinal, a bottle rack), signing it and exhibiting it as a work. If that is art, then art <strong>is no longer defined</strong> by skill, beauty or imitation: it becomes a <strong>question</strong> and a <strong>gesture</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Walter Benjamin</strong> analysed <strong>technical reproducibility</strong>: when a work can be <strong>copied</strong> endlessly (photography, cinema, now digital media), it loses its <strong>aura</strong>—its unique, almost sacred character—but becomes <strong>accessible to the masses</strong>. The image ceases to be rare and comes to be <strong>everywhere</strong>. <strong>Guy Debord</strong> spoke of the <strong>society of the spectacle</strong>: a life in which relationships pass through images and everything tends to become a spectacle to be consumed. <strong>Jean Baudrillard</strong> took the idea to the extreme with the <strong>simulacrum</strong>: copies without an original, images that no longer represent a reality but replace it (filters, advertising, virtual worlds). And the <strong>market</strong> turned art into an <strong>investment</strong>.</p>\n<p>To go further: if a perfect copy cannot be told apart from the original, why is it worth less? In the 1930s and 1940s Han van Meegeren sold paintings he had painted himself as if they were by Vermeer. In 1945, accused of having sold a Vermeer to Göring, he confessed that the painting was his and painted another in front of witnesses to prove it. The case forced the experts to ask themselves what exactly they admired. Nelson Goodman (<em>Languages of Art</em>, 1968) replied that knowing that a painting is a fake changes how we look at it, even if today we cannot see the difference. You can discuss it in the dilemma <a href=\"#dilemas/fil-falsa\">‘The painting that was a fake’</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Benjamin: the aura, more slowly.</strong> Walter Benjamin wrote “The Work of Art in the Age of Its Technological Reproducibility” in exile in Paris, and published a first version, in French, in 1936. His starting point is simple: <em>“even the most perfect reproduction lacks one thing: the here and now of the work of art, its unique existence in the place where it is”</em> (section II; own translation from the German). This here and now is what he calls the <strong>aura</strong>: the work has a history of its own (who touched it, where it has been, how it has aged) and a certain distance, even when we have it in front of us. Benjamin links the aura to the religious origin of art. The first images had <strong>cult value</strong>: what mattered was that they existed, not that they were seen, and some statues of gods were seen only by the priest. Technological reproduction pushes them towards <strong>exhibition value</strong>: what counts is that they are seen, everywhere and at once. With photography and film the question changes at the root, because a thousand copies come from a single negative and asking which one is the authentic one stops making sense. Benjamin did not simply lament the loss: he saw in it a chance for the public to stop worshipping works and start discussing them. Today the most curious case is the <em>Mona Lisa</em>: it is on millions of screens, but the room in the Louvre is still full of people jostling to see it behind glass… and almost all of them look at it through their phones. Reproduction has not destroyed the aura of the original; perhaps it has turned the original into a celebrity.</p>\n<p><strong>The Van Meegeren case and Goodman’s question.</strong> Han van Meegeren (1889-1947) was a Dutch painter scorned by the critics. In 1937 he passed off a <em>Supper at Emmaus</em> as a Vermeer: the greatest expert of the day, Abraham Bredius, declared it genuine, and the Boijmans Museum in Rotterdam bought it that same year. Van Meegeren knew what he was doing: he painted on old seventeenth-century canvases, with period pigments, and hardened the paint in the oven with a synthetic resin until it looked as if it had been dry for centuries. After the war, accused of having sold a Vermeer to Göring, he risked being tried for collaborating with the enemy; in the end he was tried for fraud, in 1947, sentenced to a year in prison, and died that same December without serving it. Today those faces look to us as if they came from the 1930s, and it is hard to believe they fooled anyone. Nelson Goodman used cases like this for a precise question: if, looking at two paintings, the original and the copy, we see no difference at all, is there any aesthetic difference between them? His answer, in paraphrase, is yes: knowing that one is a fake tells us there may be a difference we do not yet perceive, forces us to train our eye and changes what we look for in the painting. What we cannot see today a better-trained eye may see tomorrow, as Van Meegeren himself proves.</p>\n<p><strong>Autographic and allographic arts, and a series with no original.</strong> Goodman added a distinction that makes clear when it makes sense to talk of forgery. In the <strong>autographic</strong> arts, such as painting or sculpture, the work is an object with a particular history, and the only way to know whether it is genuine is to trace that history back to the artist’s hand: that is why they can be forged. In the <strong>allographic</strong> arts, such as music or literature, there is a notation (the score, the text), and any performance or copy that follows it correctly is the work. Nobody forges Beethoven’s <em>Ninth</em> by playing it, or <em>Don Quixote</em> by printing it; at most, one forges a manuscript. Printmaking lies halfway: there are many prints, and all of them are originals if they come from the artist’s plate. Andy Warhol took this idea to the extreme with screen printing. After Marilyn Monroe’s death, in August 1962, he took a publicity photograph of the actress and repeated it fifty times in the <em>Marilyn Diptych</em> (1962, now at Tate in London): twenty-five in garish colours on the left and twenty-five in black and white on the right, increasingly blurred. There is no original portrait behind the copies: the work is the repetition itself. It can be read in several ways. With Adorno, as one more commodity of the culture industry, selling a face as one sells a tin. With Debord, as spectacle in its purest form: of the woman, only the image remains. With Benjamin, ambivalently: the image no longer has an aura, but nor does it demand devotion, and it lets us see how fame is manufactured and how it fades.</p>\n<p><strong>Today: AI-made images and NFTs.</strong> The question of the original returns today in two new forms, and both remain open. The first is the image generated by artificial intelligence. In August 2022, Jason Allen won a digital art competition at the Colorado State Fair with <em>Théâtre D’opéra Spatial</em>, an image he had obtained with the program Midjourney after hundreds of attempts and then retouched. Some protested: he had not painted it. Others replied that photography, too, had been denied the status of art because the image was made by a machine, and that choosing, discarding and correcting is a form of authorship. In 2023 the United States Copyright Office refused to register it in his name, judging the part generated by the machine to be too large. There is also a deeper objection: these programs learn from millions of works by artists who never gave permission. The second form is the <strong>NFT</strong>, a digital certificate recorded on a blockchain that states who owns a file. In March 2021 the auction house Christie’s sold <em>Everydays: The First 5000 Days</em>, by Beeple, for about 69 million dollars. The paradox is that anyone can copy the file without losing a single pixel: what is bought is not a different image, but the title of ownership. For some, it is a way for digital artists to be paid for their work; for others, an attempt to manufacture an artificial aura precisely where Benjamin said there was none left. After the euphoria of 2021, the NFT market collapsed in 2022, which feeds both readings.</p>\n<p><strong>Something to think about:</strong> imagine that a friend gives you a print of your favourite painting for your birthday and that, at the same time, a gallery offers you an original by the same painter, small and less well known. Which would you hang in your room, and why? Now think of your favourite song: does it have an original, or is it the whole work every time it plays, as Goodman would say of music? If tomorrow an artificial intelligence painted a new picture in the style of Vermeer, would it be worth as much as Van Meegeren’s fakes, less or more? And finally, on limits: choose a film, a series, a video game or a song that someone has wanted to ban or withdraw. Who was asking for it, and with what reasons? What would be lost if it were withdrawn, and what would be gained? Should it be decided by a government, the company that distributes it, the public or nobody?</p>\n<p>Today the image is <strong>digital, interactive and participatory</strong>: anyone creates and shares images (social media, memes, videos), and <strong>artificial intelligence</strong> generates works that reopen the old questions—is it art? who is the author? what is original?—. That is why the topic ends where we have to live: we need <strong>visual literacy</strong>, that is, learning to <strong>look critically</strong> at the images that surround us—who makes them, what for, what they want us to feel or buy. That lucid gaze at the image is, today, one of the most urgent tasks of philosophy.</p>\n<p><strong>The art market: a critical analysis.</strong> Today art is also a global market. Some works sell for hundreds of millions, and the great museums of contemporary art (Tate, MoMA, the Guggenheim) are also brands, tourist magnets and businesses. This raises problems: the price of a work often depends on speculation more than on its artistic value; a few artists become hugely famous while most can barely make a living from their work; and the conversation shifts from what the work says to what it is worth. The most telling case came from Banksy in October 2018: his <em>Girl with Balloon</em> sold at a Sotheby's auction in London for over a million pounds and, just after the hammer fell, a shredder hidden in the frame cut it halfway through. It looked like a joke at the market's expense, but the work, renamed <em>Love is in the Bin</em>, was sold again in 2021 for more than eighteen million. The market also has its good side, because it spreads works around and gives artists visibility; the risk is that art is reduced to an economic object.</p>\n<h2>Minimal dictionary</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr> <th><strong>Term</strong></th> <th>Meaning</th> </tr> </thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr> <td>Aesthetics</td> <td>Branch of philosophy that reflects on beauty, art and aesthetic experience.</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Aisthesis</td> <td>‘Sensation, perception’; Greek root of ‘aesthetics’ (sensory knowledge).</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Aesthetic experience</td> <td>Disinterested perception in which we contemplate something for its own sake.</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Objective / subjective beauty</td> <td>Beauty is in the object (proportion) / in the subject (taste).</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>The sublime</td> <td>Feeling of fear and admiration before the immense or the powerful.</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Judgement of taste</td> <td>Judgement that values something as beautiful.</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Disinterestedness (Kant)</td> <td>Contemplating without wanting to possess or use the thing.</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Mimesis</td> <td>Imitation or representation of reality.</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Catharsis</td> <td>Purification of the emotions produced by tragedy (Aristotle).</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Techne / ars</td> <td>‘Know-how’; common origin of art and technique.</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Fine arts</td> <td>Arts whose purpose is aesthetic, not useful (painting, music, poetry…).</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Genius</td> <td>Original artist who creates new rules.</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Institutional theory</td> <td>Something is art because the ‘art world’ recognises it (Danto, Dickie).</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Ready-made</td> <td>Ordinary object turned into a work by exhibiting it (Duchamp).</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Aura</td> <td>Unique, unrepeatable character of the work, lost when it is reproduced (Benjamin).</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Society of the spectacle</td> <td>Life mediated by images that are consumed (Debord).</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Simulacrum</td> <td>Image without an original, which replaces reality (Baudrillard).</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Moralism / autonomism</td> <td>Morality does / does not affect the artistic value of the work.</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Visual literacy</td> <td>Knowing how to look critically at images.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>"}});
